# Copy to .env and set AI_API_KEY. Nothing else has to be set to start. Existing OS env
# wins over this file, so a systemd unit can override it without editing anything.
#
# What is here: the keys you have a decision to make about. What is not: the fourteen that sit
# at their own defaults — they are named at the bottom, `internal/config` owns their values,
# and a running instance prints every one with the value it resolved to and where that came
# from at /#/admin. This file used to spell all of them out, which is the second copy of a
# number that goes stale first.

# --- provider (OpenAI) -------------------------------------------------------
# Any OpenAI-compatible base URL works (Azure, Groq, OpenRouter) — that is a property of the
# wire format, not a feature this repo maintains. Point AI_BASE_URL at a new one and run
# `make live`: it reports in seconds whether it serves /embeddings too, which is the one gap
# that stops ingest dead. Split EMBED_BASE_URL / EMBED_API_KEY off it ONLY when the chat
# provider has no /embeddings — a key is not valid at two vendors, so splitting the URL
# without the key just fails auth.
AI_API_KEY=sk-...

# --- models ------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this instance will answer with, and what the settings drawer offers a reader. First
# entry is the default; window and prices are optional per entry, and an entry that omits them
# simply prints no percentage and no cost. Unset means the single model CHAT_MODEL names.
# CHAT_MODELS=gpt-4o-mini:128000:0.15:0.60,gpt-4o:128000:2.50:10.00
#             └ model      └ window └ $/1M in └ $/1M out

# One pair has to agree: EMBED_DIM must equal what EMBED_MODEL returns (3-small 1536 ·
# 3-large 3072). This client sends no `dimensions` field, so a shortened width fails on
# insert — and changing the embedding model needs a full re-ingest, because vectors from two
# models are not comparable. Change neither and there is nothing to set here.

# --- hosting -----------------------------------------------------------------
# BIND_ADDR is loopback, deliberately: whoever reaches the port reads every indexed
# document. Publish it with `tailscale serve` or a Cloudflare Tunnel rather than opening it.

# Optional HTTP Basic auth over everything except /api/health. Empty = no auth.
AUTH_USER=team
AUTH_PASS=

# The admin gate. Set it to open /#/admin — a read-only screen listing every setting with
# the value it resolved to and where that came from (.env, the shell, or the default), which
# is the one thing you cannot work out by reading files. Empty means that screen and its
# route do not exist. Its own password, not BA_PASS: the list reports which secrets are set,
# so "may publish an answer" and "may read the shape of every secret" stay separate.
ADMIN_PASS=

# The BA write gate — confirm and dismiss need it; asking and reading never do.
# Empty means this instance has NO write surface, and BA mode says so up front.
# Keep it different from AUTH_PASS: readers share that one.
BA_PASS=

# --- storage -----------------------------------------------------------------
# DB_PATH is the corpus, not an index: documents live in it, text and all, and the BA screen
# is the only way one enters. Losing that file loses the knowledge base, so it is the one
# path `scripts/backup.sh` reads: `make backup` snapshots it off this machine's disk, and a
# systemd timer runs the same script nightly. Run it before touching anything here.
# CORPUS_DIR is only the folder `ingest` reads when an operator imports from disk — nothing
# writes there, so read access is enough.

# --- the fourteen already at their defaults -----------------------------------
# AI_BASE_URL · EMBED_BASE_URL · EMBED_API_KEY · CHAT_MODEL · CHAT_MODELS · EMBED_MODEL ·
# EMBED_DIM ·
# TOP_K · BIND_ADDR · PORT · CORPUS_DIR · DB_PATH · CONTEXT_WINDOW · PRICE_IN · PRICE_OUT
#
# Add any of them here to override. The status-line three are zero on purpose: an unmeasured
# cost and a cost of nothing are different facts, so zero hides the figure rather than
# printing it, and token counts come from the provider's usage frame with no config at all.
